From 181ba7d569bb62e5720b02b136b2a698c157ca48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:18:18 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] dsv41: TF_DS_ENGRAM_BOTH (off by default) lands both
 Engram layers' rows at the round's first Engram cut - three stretches a round
 become two

Switch: TF_DS_ENGRAM_BOTH (1 or on; off otherwise). Read once, at Round.init, the same way TF_DS_COMPRESS_SIDE is read (set alike on both ranks; no fingerprint bit, no switches bit: no frames). Only honored with exactly two Engram layers (the family's [1, 14]); any other count keeps the base path.

Today the round's stretch loop is cut before every Engram layer: layer 0 (stretch 1), layers 1..13 (stretch 2, landing layer 1's rows before it), layers 14..39 and the head (stretch 3, landing layer 14's rows before it). The reads (AIO or the reader pool) are submitted for all layers at once, but the rows' host decode and upload still happen in two landings, and the cut before layer 14 exists only to give the host its second window. With the switch on:

- both Engram layers' rows are decoded and uploaded at the round's first Engram cut (before stretch 2 of 2, i.e. before layer 1's stretch), in config slot order (slot 0 = the first Engram layer, slot 1 = the second); layer 14's rows are strictly earlier than today, never later;
- the cut before layer 14 is gone: stretch 2 runs layers 1..39 and the head in one go (end == layers.len, so stretch() runs the head as it does today);
- graphs mode captures 2 stretches a round instead of 3 (the stretch index is part of the capture key, so nothing else moves); eager loses one stretch boundary (one host landing and one clock wait);
- the phase profile's engram time shows no change: the landings overlap the first stretch either way (today layer 14's happens before stretch 3 of 3; now both happen before stretch 2 of 2). A prediction, not a measurement - no pair run here.

Two device slots: rd.e_in (slot 0, as today) and a new rd.e_in2 (slot 1, sized by the second Engram layer's own ew.k, so a differing pair of layers is fine). With the switch off rd.e_in2 stays 0 and the stretch's Engram block takes rd.e_in as before - no behavior change. The OFF allocation path keeps the base shape (the pointer overwritten per Engram layer, the first buffer orphaned in the arena - arena bytes, unchanged here); with the switch on both slots are kept (one take per Engram layer).

Landing flow: engramLand is now a dispatcher. With the switch on and ah.both unset, the first Engram layer's landing lands every Engram layer's rows (engramLandSlot, the old body per slot: wait ah.ids[j] (AIO, slot order 0 then 1), decodeRow, upload to the slot's buffer) and sets ah.both; later calls in the same round are no-ops (forward also skips them). The AIO waits are sequential on the same stream and are deliberately not overlapped - correctness first. engramAhead is unchanged (it always submitted both layers). With the switch off, engramLand does exactly what it did (slot j into rd.e_in; the switch-on fallback for a lone second-layer landing picks e_in2).

Bits: identical (same decodeRow, same uploads, same bytes to the same kernels; the differences are timing - layer 14's landing moved earlier - and buffer placement - two slots instead of one reused in place). Expected on the pair: exact.py IDENTICAL; eager GRAPHS=0 and graphs TF_DS_GRAPHS=1 are both worth one chat smoke before it graduates past a measurement.

Not this patch: staging the rows to the device (the E-proper design, still open - the host still lands once, between the first stretch and the rest); removing the host step. The design note for staging is a separate document.

Judgement calls: (1) the task brief described the base e_in allocation as a double take overwriting the pointer (first orphaned); the actual base loop breaks after the first Engram layer (one take). The implementation keeps the brief's described loop with the OFF else-branch overwriting the pointer - i.e. OFF now orphans one buffer relative to the code that was actually there; arena bytes, no exposed difference. (2) slot j maps to the j-th entry of c.engram_layers (the config's engram_layer_ids order, [1, 14] ascending as written) - the same order indexOfScalar already implied. (3) the fallback for a lone second-layer landing with the switch on picks e_in2; with the switch off it is always rd.e_in.

Host gates: zig build test (one reported spurious "failed command" - the pre-existing flaky KV-shard binary; it passes standalone, 409/409 OK with the reported seed, and the second run exited 0; a deterministic full sweep of .zig-cache/o was not re-run), and zig build install native -Dfatbins=/home/colum/.cache/tf-dummy-fatbins succeeds (the CUDA path type-checks; no .cu files changed in this patch, so no clang stub check was needed).

Not run here: any pair, any smoke, exact.py, graphs capture. The graphs/capture interplay (2 stretches a round instead of 3 under TF_DS_GRAPHS=1) and the profile prediction above are unverified.

